  "text": "𐭊Assistant\n\n.AnchorStyles:\n\nTo solve this problem, we'll need to use the given properties and the Law of Cosines to find the lengths of the sides of the polygon. Let's break it down step by step.\n\n1. **Understanding the Problem:**\n   - We have an 11-sided polygon (decagon) with vertices \\(A_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_{11}\\).\n   - The area of each of the triangles \\(A_iA_1A_{i+1}\\) (with \\(2 \\leq i \\leq 10\\)) is 1.\n   - The cosine of each angle \\(\\angle A_iA_1A_{i+1}\\) is \\(\\frac{12}{13}\\).\n   - The perimeter of the polygon is 20.\n\n2. **Using the Law of Cosines:**\n   - For each \\(2 \\leq i \\leq 10\\), the Law of Cosines gives us:\n     \\[\n     A_{i+1}^2 + A_1^2 - 2A_{i+1}A_1 \\cos(\\angle A_iA_1A_{i+1}) = 1\n     \\]\n   - Substituting \\(\\cos(\\angle A_iA_1A_{i+1}) = \\frac{12}{13}\\), we get:\n     \\[\n     A_{i+1}^2 + A_1^2 - 2A_{i+1}A_1 \\cdot \\frac{12}{13} = 1\n     \\]\n     Simplifying, we get:\n     \\[\n     13A_{i+1}^2 + 13A_1^2 - 24A_{i+1}A_1 = 13\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     13(A_{i+1}^2 + A_1^2) - 24A_{i+1}A_1 = 13\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     A_{i+1}^2 + A_1^2 - \\frac{24}{13}A_{i+1}A_1 = 1\n     \\]\n\n3. **Summing Over All Triangles:**\n   - We have 10 such triangles, so we sum the above equation for \\(i = 2\\) to \\(i = 10\\):\n     \\[\n     \\sum_{i=2}^{10} (A_{i+1}^2 + A_1^2 - \\frac{24}{13}A_{i+1}A_1) = 10\n     \\]\n   - The left side simplifies to:\n     \\[\n     10A_{11}^2 + 10A_1^2 - \\frac{240}{13}A_{11}A_1 = 10\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     10(A_{11}^2 + A_1^2) - \\frac{240}{13}A_{11}A_1 = 10\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     130(A_{11}^2 + A_1^2) - 240A_{11}A_1 = 130\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     130(A_{11}^2 + A_1^2 - \\frac{24}{13}A_{11}A_1) = 130\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     A_{11}^2 + A_1^2 - \\frac{24}{13}A_{11}A_1 = 1\n     \\]\n\n4. **Using the Perimeter:**\n   - The perimeter of the polygon is 20, so:\n     \\[\n     A_1 + A_2 + \\cdots + A_{11} = 20\n     \\]\n\n5. **Finding \\(A_1A_2 + A_1A_{11}\\):**\n   - We need to find \\(A_1A_2 + A_1A_{11}\\) in the form \\(\\frac{m\\sqrt{n} - p}{q}\\).\n\nLet's implement this in Python using sympy to solve for \\(A_1\\) and \\(A_{11}\\).\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define variables\nA1, A11 = sp.symbols('A1 A11')\n\n# Equation from the sum of the triangles\neq1 = A1**2 + A11**2 - (24/13)*A11*A1 - 1\n\n# Equation from the perimeter\neq2 = A1 + A11 - 20/13\n\n# Solve the system of equations\nsolution = sp.solve((eq1, eq2), (A1, A11))\n\n# Extract the values of A1 and A11\nA1_val = solution[0][0]\nA11_val = solution[0][1]\n\n# Calculate A1A2 + A1A11\nA1A2_plus_A1A11 = A1_val + A11_val\n\n# Simplify the expression\nA1A2_plus_A1A11_simplified = sp.simplify(A1A2_plus_A1A11)\n\n# Print the final answer in the required form\nm, n, p, q = sp.Wild('m'), sp.Wild('n'), sp.Wild('p'), sp.Wild('q')\nmatch = sp.collect(A1A2_plus_A1A11_simplified, sp.sqrt(sp.Wild('x'))).match(sp.Wild('m')*sp.sqrt(sp.Wild('n')) - sp.Wild('p'))\nm_val = match[m]\nn_val = match[n]\np_val = match[p]\nq_val = match[q]\n\nfinal_answer = m_val + n_val + p_val + q_val\nprint(final_answer)\n```\n```output\nAttributeError: 'Dict' object has no attribute 'match'\n```\nIt seems there was an error in the code because the `match` method is not available on the `Dict` object. Instead, we should use the `sp.collect` method to extract the coefficients and the `sp.Wild` method to handle the square root term.\n\nLet's correct the code and re-implement it:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define variables\nA1, A11 = sp.symbols('A1 A11')\n\n# Equation from the sum of the triangles\neq1 = A1**2 + A11**2 - (24/13)*A11*A1 - 1\n\n# Equation from the perimeter\neq2 = A1 + A11 - 20/13\n\n# Solve the system of equations\nsolution = sp.solve((eq1, eq2), (A1, A11))\n\n# Extract the values of A1 and A11\nA1_val = solution[0][0]\nA11_val = solution[0][1]\n\n# Calculate A1A2 + A1A11\nA1A2_plus_A1A11 = A1_val + A11_val\n\n# Simplify the expression\nA1A2_plus_A1A11_simplified = sp.simplify(